================
VLAN Segmentation

Why we did this
================
Initially, all devices in a network were placed in the same VLAN and broadcast domain.

As organizations grew, this created several problems:

- Excessive broadcast traffic
- Poor network performance
- Lack of department isolation
- Increased security risks
- Easy lateral movement for attackers

For example:

Finance PC, HR PC, Server, and Guest Laptop were all in the same network.

If an attacker compromised one device, they could potentially reach all other devices.

To solve this problem, VLAN Segmentation was introduced.

VLAN Segmentation logically divides a physical switch into multiple isolated networks.

What will happen
================
1. Devices are grouped into separate VLANs.
2. Each VLAN becomes its own broadcast domain.
3. Broadcast traffic stays within that VLAN.
4. Departments become isolated from each other.
5. Communication between VLANs requires a Layer 3 device.

Example:

VLAN 10 = Finance
VLAN 20 = HR
VLAN 30 = IT
VLAN 40 = Guest

Finance users can communicate within VLAN 10.

HR users can communicate within VLAN 20.

Direct communication between VLANs is not possible unless routing is configured.

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Improves security through isolation.
2. Reduces broadcast traffic.
3. Improves network performance.
4. Makes network management easier.
5. Limits lateral movement of attackers.
6. Supports departmental separation.
7. Provides logical network segmentation without additional hardware.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Devices in different VLANs cannot communicate directly.
2. Requires VLAN planning and management.
3. Misconfigured VLANs can cause connectivity issues.
4. Does not prevent attacks within the same VLAN.
5. Inter-VLAN communication requires routing.

Hence what came next
--------------------
After implementing VLAN Segmentation, administrators noticed that some devices inside the same VLAN still needed isolation.

For example:

Guest Room 1 PC and Guest Room 2 PC belong to the same VLAN.

Even though they are in the same VLAN, they should not communicate with each other.

This led to:

Private VLAN (PVLAN)

which provides isolation between devices within the same VLAN.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Switch(config)# vlan 10
Switch(config-vlan)# name FINANCE

Switch(config)# vlan 20
Switch(config-vlan)# name HR

Switch(config)# vlan 30
Switch(config-vlan)# name IT

Switch(config)# vlan 40
Switch(config-vlan)# name GUEST

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode access
Switch(config-if)# switchport access vlan 10

Switch(config)# interface fa0/2
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode access
Switch(config-if)# switchport access vlan 20

Switch(config)# interface fa0/3
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode access
Switch(config-if)# switchport access vlan 30

Switch(config)# interface fa0/4
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode access
Switch(config-if)# switchport access vlan 40

Switch(config)# end

Verification
============

show vlan brief

show interfaces switchport

Expected Result
===============

Finance Users:
--------------
Can communicate with Finance VLAN devices.

HR Users:
---------
Can communicate with HR VLAN devices.

IT Users:
---------
Can communicate with IT VLAN devices.

Guest Users:
------------
Remain within Guest VLAN.

Inter-VLAN Communication:
-------------------------
Not possible without Layer 3 routing.

Summary
=======

Problem:
All devices were in the same network, creating security and performance issues.

Solution:
VLAN Segmentation separates devices into multiple isolated broadcast domains.

Benefit:
Improves security, performance, and network organization.

Limitation:
Devices within the same VLAN can still communicate freely.

